%a.header
  Pumpfoiling
%div.chapter
  %p
    I started pumpfoiling late 2024 and since then its my favorite sport.
    Somehow I knew it from the start after seeing the <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20230727212647/https://betafoils.com/">betafoils page</a> back then.
    But I could not have guessed how deeply it impacted me. Not sure why, perhaps its the quiet lake sessions, perhaps
    its the powder like glide that you get. No matter why its an awesome sport and it will makes me a happier human being!
  %br

  %a.header
    The beginnings
  %div.chapter
    %p
      It was very hard at the beginning, so hard that I thought how can anybody invent that! So thanks to all the pumpfoiling pioneers!
    %br

    %a.header Mistakes
    %div.chapter
      %p
        While its a bit negative to start from mistakes, I think knowing that something is wrong is the first path to doing the right thing.
        Here are a few things that I would do now differently at the beginning or that I would recommend to follow, as they make the beginning much easier.
        %ul
          %li
            Use an high enough dock (first chapter of the <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20260815210343/https://betafoils.com/blogs/training-guide">betafoils training guide</a>). Ideal is almost mast height, so that you get the best initial glide and dont have to jump high onto the board.
          %li
            I still think that the <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WSXoRP9TB3U">inverse start</a> by Yvonite is the most approachable that gives you a fast first feeling of success. The saver bet, that is maybe more flexible is the <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20260815210343/https://betafoils.com/blogs/training-guide">kneestart</a>.
          %li
            Get a dock with a ladder or even better with steps, because you have to get back on the dock a lot.
          %li
            If possible select a lake that is not very wide, that way you can always swim to shore and walk back. While its a good training to paddle, we want to learn to pump and not to paddle.
          %li
            If turns at the beginning are a problem, try to connect beaches in a straight line so that you can walk back instead of swim back.
          %li
            Take it slow. Different parts of the body adjust with different speed for the new strain on the body, and while maybe the muscles adjust very fast, the tendons and cartilages might need a bit longer. See the section about injuries.
      %br

    %a.header Thoughts
    %div.chapter
      %p
        After you get comfortable on the foil (and complete the <a href="https://www.wakethief.com/1min-challenge">1 minute</a>, <a href="https://betafoils.com/pages/freefoil10k">10k</a> challenge)
        something changes and you might observe a few strange thoughts:
        %ul
          %li
            You will ask on every shoreline: Can I start from here?
          %li
            You will get interested in your heartrate and maybe even consider zone 2 cardio training, because all the
            long distance pumpers promote it.
                
    %a.header Injuries
    %div.chapter
      %p
        I do not know too many pumpfoilers (yet) but two of them (including me) got a bone bruise from intense training.

